.TH VELVET 7 2026-08-03 Velvet "Velvet Manual"
.SH NAME
velvet \- GitHub-native status monitoring and status pages
.SH DESCRIPTION
Velvet monitors websites and HTTP endpoints from GitHub Actions, records
incidents and planned maintenance in GitHub Issues, and publishes a themeable
status page through GitHub Pages. It uses no server and no database. A public
website is described by a display name and a URL, and every other option has a
default.
.PP
An installation is an ordinary GitHub repository. What Velvet adds to it is a
set of workflows, a configuration file named
.IR velvet.yml ,
a machine-managed version record named
.IR velvet.lock.json ,
and a dedicated branch holding the generated data.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
Every moving part of an installation is a service GitHub already provides, so
none of the following is hosted by the project.
.TP
.B Scheduling
GitHub Actions. The status workflow runs every five minutes, and the
response-time workflow runs at 00:00, 06:00, 12:00, and 18:00 UTC.
.TP
.B Incidents and maintenance
GitHub Issues. A confirmed outage opens one marked Issue, and a confirmed
recovery closes the same Issue. Planned maintenance is submitted through a
generated Issue Form.
.TP
.B Generated data
A dedicated branch named
.BR velvet-data .
The monitor owns four files there and never rewrites the default branch.
.TP
.B The public site
GitHub Pages, optionally under a custom domain.
.SH HOW AN INSTALLATION WORKS
.IP 1. 4
.I velvet.yml
names the repository, the page, and every service. The repository owner and
name in it must match the repository the workflow runs in, and a mismatch stops
the run before any check or repository change.
.IP 2. 4
The monitor checks every configured endpoint over IPv4 from a GitHub-hosted
runner. Each check gets one initial attempt and at most one immediate retry.
.IP 3. 4
A successful run publishes one complete validated snapshot to the
.B velvet-data
branch. A partial or failed result never replaces the last valid snapshot.
.IP 4. 4
The Pages workflow builds the site, its social card, and its SEO files from
that snapshot.
.IP 5. 4
The browser validates the published documents before rendering them. Endpoint
URLs and configured secrets never enter them.
.SH INSTALLATION
The browser setup at
.UR https://setup.velvet.li/onboarding/
setup.velvet.li
.UE
is the only supported way to install Velvet. It asks for the repository and
page name, the services, an optional custom domain, and one of the four system
themes, and it creates the repository, enables Pages, and starts monitoring
once the GitHub App is approved.
.PP
It is also the only path that writes
.IR velvet.lock.json ,
the record of which release an installation runs. A repository created by
copying the template by hand carries no such record, so Velvet has no version
to compare against and can never update it.
.PP
The setup service is used only whilst installing and whilst updating. A
published status page keeps monitoring and publishing whilst the service is
unavailable.
.SH CONFIGURATOR
The Configurator edits
.I velvet.yml
and previews the real status page. It runs in two places.
.PP
The hosted one at
.UR https://setup.velvet.li/configurator/
setup.velvet.li/configurator
.UE
signs in with GitHub, finds the installations you administer, shows which
release each one runs, and installs a new one for you.
.PP
The local one is described in
.BR velvet-config (1).
It edits a file on your computer and talks to no network at all, so it reports
no installed version rather than one it cannot check.
.SH UPDATES
Velvet installs new releases for you. An update replaces only the workflow and
Issue-template files Velvet owns, plus its own version record. Your
.IR velvet.yml ,
the whole
.B velvet-data
branch, your incidents, your maintenance history, repository secrets, Pages and
domain settings,
.IR README.md ,
and
.I LICENSE
are never part of one.
.PP
That boundary is checked twice. The service reads the changed files of its own
pull request before merging and stops whilst the installation is untouched if
any path falls outside the closed set, and a workflow in the repository proves
it again against the merge GitHub actually built.
.PP
A release classified as a security fix that needs neither a configuration nor a
data migration may install unattended. The preference is
.B updates.automaticSecurityUpdates
in
.BR velvet.yml (5)
and defaults to being enabled. Everything else waits for confirmation.
.SH GENERATED DATA
The monitor owns exactly these paths on the
.B velvet-data
branch and nothing else.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\&.velvet/monitor-state.json
velvet-data/v1/status.json
velvet-data/v1/response-times.json
velvet-data/v1/incidents.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Retention defaults to 365 days, which is also the maximum. Open incidents and
scheduled or active maintenance stay visible past it, and historical GitHub
Issues are never deleted.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Velvet performs direct HTTP and HTTPS checks over IPv4, because GitHub-hosted
runners provide no documented IPv6 connectivity. The configuration offers no
external-probe option, so a configured service is monitored over IPv4. IPv6
will be added once those runners support it for every installation.
.PP
An installation's history begins with the first check it runs.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I velvet.yml
The configuration, described in
.BR velvet.yml (5).
.TP
.I velvet.lock.json
The machine-managed record of the installed release. Written by the setup
service and never edited by hand.
.TP
.I .github/workflows/
The Velvet status, response-time, and Pages workflows.
.TP
.I .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/maintenance.yml
The generated maintenance Issue Form. Its service choices are rewritten
whenever the configured services change.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR velvet-config (1),
.BR velvet.yml (5)
.PP
The complete documentation is at
.UR https://velvet.li
velvet.li
.UE
and in the
.I documentation
directory of the repository.
